<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Sumedang Larang</h1><p class="st-meta">1529 CE – 1620 CE (approx.) · capital Kutamaya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sundanese kingdom in the highlands of West Java that claimed the heritage of the Sunda Kingdom before submitting to Mataram in 1620.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumedang">Sumedang</a> Larang was a principality in the Priangan highlands, long subordinate to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom</a>. Its rulers accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the 16th century, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangeran-santri">Pangeran Santri</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan Pajajaran</a> in 1579, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> nobles are said to have carried the royal crown, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binokasih">Binokasih</a>, to Sumedang.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prabu-geusan-ulun">Prabu Geusan Ulun</a>, who received the regalia, claimed to rule as heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> over much of the Priangan, from his capital at Kutamaya. The realm was agricultural, based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming, and kept Sundanese court traditions while adopting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Squeezed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a> and the expanding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumedang">Sumedang</a> submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-agung">Sultan Agung of Mataram</a> in 1620, and its ruler became a regent. It later passed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binokasih">Binokasih crown</a> is kept today in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prabu-geusan-ulun-museum">Prabu Geusan Ulun Museum</a> in Sumedang.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sumedang-larang.jpg" alt="Sumedang Larang" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Nicolaes Visscher II · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolaes_Visscher_Indiae_Orientalis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1529 CE – 1620 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kutamaya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pangeran Santri, Geusan Ulun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sundanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Sumedang Larang</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1574&amp;focus=sumedang-larang" aria-label="Sumedang Larang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7495 2220 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sumedang Larang lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8052 2725h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7815 2476h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7815 2476h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Indonesia. <a href="/atlas?year=1574&amp;focus=sumedang-larang">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
